2 Scheme Quickstart for Sake Users
**********************************

You do *not* need prior knowledge of Scheme or functional programming to
write ‘sakefile.scm’ files.  Sake uses a tiny subset of Scheme.

2.1 Parentheses and Procedure Calls
===================================

In Scheme, operations are written inside parentheses, with the
action/procedure first:

     ;; In C/Shell: cc = "gcc"
     ;; In Scheme(sake):
     (sakevar cc "gcc")

     ;; Calling a procedure: (procedure-name arg1 arg2)
     (target "main.o" #:deps '("main.c") #:cmd "gcc -c main.c -o main.o")

2.2 Data Types Used in Sake
===========================

   • *Strings*: Enclosed in double quotes, e.g., ‘"gcc"’ or ‘"main.o"’.
   • *Lists*: A collection of items preceded by a single quote, e.g.,
     ‘'("file1.c" "file2.c")’ or ‘'()’.
   • *Keywords*: Words starting with ‘#:’, used for named arguments like
     ‘#:deps’ and ‘#:cmd’.


